[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186124467 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2986a890-b241-5a06-ba6c-578dfc9139a6" "question-ecf33790-880b-54f0-875d-ea7461ed14bb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124271 :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find the limit $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).$ \n\n![](images/49/4982eb6e9020e5f6235049fd36a030a69d7b74a0e3ea60323ac8887f4c9bca79.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that as $x$ increases to $\\infty,$ the graph of the function moves closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=4.$\n\nTherefore, as $x$ approaches $\\infty,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches $4,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty } f(x) = 4.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124579 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9e0ab428-de64-5dc9-82ec-d9e388ec395a" "question-20650e5d-9531-58b0-8986-a410d28ac857" "question-be5e5f56-4a60-5c32-8894-f56ae09b72b4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124193 :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find  $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} f(x).$ \n\n![](images/c7/c7ee917a93ed51c189979516b9ab0d3232c183921ee277f522f617518ce03fdf.png)" :question/worked-solution "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty$, the graph of the function moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=2.$ \n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ approaches the value $2$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -\\infty } f(x) = 2.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty,$ the graph of the function grows without bound to $\\infty$ as well. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\infty } f(x) = \\infty.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-2986a890-b241-5a06-ba6c-578dfc9139a6" :question/id #uuid "2986a890-b241-5a06-ba6c-578dfc9139a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-337600" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b35bd1000242bcbc00fcd5e1cd04d8f3b13e563f2df72234b438827949ad4ef5.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Therefore\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that as $x$ decreases to $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function moves closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches $2,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bebf9261-924a-5e1a-88b8-24fd5ab23e17"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bebf9261-924a-5e1a-88b8-24fd5ab23e17" :answer-field/id #uuid "bebf9261-924a-5e1a-88b8-24fd5ab23e17" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1104f803-e925-52a4-a645-afee67efaa1e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1104f803-e925-52a4-a645-afee67efaa1e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1104f803-e925-52a4-a645-afee67efaa1e" :answer/id #uuid "1104f803-e925-52a4-a645-afee67efaa1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ecf33790-880b-54f0-875d-ea7461ed14bb" :question/id #uuid "ecf33790-880b-54f0-875d-ea7461ed14bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-35812" :question/problem "![](images/d6/d6448b3b7899a447d4259d6e4d60ea33262306755485aafa7604e13b3709fca0.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that as $x$ decreases to $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function moves closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=-2.$\n\nTherefore, as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches $-2,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=-2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-040f1458-944f-575a-95cf-2a20a2ad718a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-040f1458-944f-575a-95cf-2a20a2ad718a" :answer-field/id #uuid "040f1458-944f-575a-95cf-2a20a2ad718a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1b222c95-61f8-57e8-8e0f-3573a20419c6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1b222c95-61f8-57e8-8e0f-3573a20419c6" "answer-cb74d9a8-f4e5-5ef1-bcfb-8deb3748409e" "answer-777f48fb-1b50-589b-a9a3-8a2623788e9a" "answer-b2785696-f2d6-51fd-8b99-47190908ed88" "answer-9d4ed3c0-e5e6-53eb-bdc3-642a46c636f4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b222c95-61f8-57e8-8e0f-3573a20419c6" :answer/id #uuid "1b222c95-61f8-57e8-8e0f-3573a20419c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb74d9a8-f4e5-5ef1-bcfb-8deb3748409e" :answer/id #uuid "cb74d9a8-f4e5-5ef1-bcfb-8deb3748409e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-777f48fb-1b50-589b-a9a3-8a2623788e9a" :answer/id #uuid "777f48fb-1b50-589b-a9a3-8a2623788e9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2785696-f2d6-51fd-8b99-47190908ed88" :answer/id #uuid "b2785696-f2d6-51fd-8b99-47190908ed88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d4ed3c0-e5e6-53eb-bdc3-642a46c636f4" :answer/id #uuid "9d4ed3c0-e5e6-53eb-bdc3-642a46c636f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9e0ab428-de64-5dc9-82ec-d9e388ec395a" :question/id #uuid "9e0ab428-de64-5dc9-82ec-d9e388ec395a" :question/math-academy-id "q-337625" :question/problem "![](images/71/717e9728ec64332ffa0f115b8698fe5babac2b6c11e1764aeb28494b89a0c141.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Therefore\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} \n$\\,$ and $\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty $, the graph of the function moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=3.$ \n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ approaches the value $3$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=3.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$ the graph of the function goes without bound to $-\\infty .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7bb33194-2026-55f0-9bc6-4eaf63699f95" "answer-field-b8a64255-f025-5f63-b69a-4e7b54b34285"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7bb33194-2026-55f0-9bc6-4eaf63699f95" :answer-field/id #uuid "7bb33194-2026-55f0-9bc6-4eaf63699f95" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f98bb391-8a6f-5d3f-9c06-ff3bb2ee4d9a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f98bb391-8a6f-5d3f-9c06-ff3bb2ee4d9a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f98bb391-8a6f-5d3f-9c06-ff3bb2ee4d9a" :answer/id #uuid "f98bb391-8a6f-5d3f-9c06-ff3bb2ee4d9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b8a64255-f025-5f63-b69a-4e7b54b34285" :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a64255-f025-5f63-b69a-4e7b54b34285" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-fe1fed8a-f0ff-54d2-b48c-4e615c5eab79" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fe1fed8a-f0ff-54d2-b48c-4e615c5eab79"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe1fed8a-f0ff-54d2-b48c-4e615c5eab79" :answer/id #uuid "fe1fed8a-f0ff-54d2-b48c-4e615c5eab79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-20650e5d-9531-58b0-8986-a410d28ac857" :question/id #uuid "20650e5d-9531-58b0-8986-a410d28ac857" :question/math-academy-id "q-102473" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d3d381842539a44e53d77697b18f0cb51552996c885a41e6681d56cba5025428.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find  ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty $, the graph of the function moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=0.$ \n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ approaches the value $0$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$ the graph of the function grows without bound to $\\infty $ as well. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d514cf12-4a07-554b-a399-ec37796f1ec8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d514cf12-4a07-554b-a399-ec37796f1ec8" :answer-field/id #uuid "d514cf12-4a07-554b-a399-ec37796f1ec8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-475186f4-dc87-58a6-9081-13a54b4a2d9c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d3886d3c-89e6-5a05-8ffc-24fd67a5d7b2" "answer-3c61804f-0426-5bd0-879c-10e37f3d6812" "answer-609c690a-1985-5867-88ea-6530c846e51c" "answer-81c2b701-5db9-575d-9e08-953094b78ec5" "answer-475186f4-dc87-58a6-9081-13a54b4a2d9c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3886d3c-89e6-5a05-8ffc-24fd67a5d7b2" :answer/id #uuid "d3886d3c-89e6-5a05-8ffc-24fd67a5d7b2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c61804f-0426-5bd0-879c-10e37f3d6812" :answer/id #uuid "3c61804f-0426-5bd0-879c-10e37f3d6812" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty $" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-609c690a-1985-5867-88ea-6530c846e51c" :answer/id #uuid "609c690a-1985-5867-88ea-6530c846e51c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=0$ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty $" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81c2b701-5db9-575d-9e08-953094b78ec5" :answer/id #uuid "81c2b701-5db9-575d-9e08-953094b78ec5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=-\\infty $" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-475186f4-dc87-58a6-9081-13a54b4a2d9c" :answer/id #uuid "475186f4-dc87-58a6-9081-13a54b4a2d9c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-be5e5f56-4a60-5c32-8894-f56ae09b72b4" :question/id #uuid "be5e5f56-4a60-5c32-8894-f56ae09b72b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-102470" :question/problem "![](images/07/07b3922c7b14ed6bb5c613f81485fc4e155232fe0958805b9437c7ca1b4e608a.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find  ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty $, the graph of the function grows without bound to $\\infty .$\n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ approaches $\\infty $ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$\nthe graph of the function moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-2.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ea243ce2-45fd-5cec-817c-777d580842aa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ea243ce2-45fd-5cec-817c-777d580842aa" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea243ce2-45fd-5cec-817c-777d580842aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4deb45f9-98ed-520e-8fe2-1fe96ff3f473" :answer-field/choices ["answer-432e6bf4-e493-5434-ae9a-e0016232e17c" "answer-31b42c29-5b27-5782-9407-a827fe74eada" "answer-4deb45f9-98ed-520e-8fe2-1fe96ff3f473" "answer-cd2fc4cf-c91f-5e2b-ba44-7663cb5c839f" "answer-dae4ec75-3a91-5668-b380-0a4af9b966a7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-432e6bf4-e493-5434-ae9a-e0016232e17c" :answer/id #uuid "432e6bf4-e493-5434-ae9a-e0016232e17c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-2$ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=-\\infty $" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31b42c29-5b27-5782-9407-a827fe74eada" :answer/id #uuid "31b42c29-5b27-5782-9407-a827fe74eada" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-2$ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4deb45f9-98ed-520e-8fe2-1fe96ff3f473" :answer/id #uuid "4deb45f9-98ed-520e-8fe2-1fe96ff3f473" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-2$ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty $" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd2fc4cf-c91f-5e2b-ba44-7663cb5c839f" :answer/id #uuid "cd2fc4cf-c91f-5e2b-ba44-7663cb5c839f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=0$ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty $" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dae4ec75-3a91-5668-b380-0a4af9b966a7" :answer/id #uuid "dae4ec75-3a91-5668-b380-0a4af9b966a7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=-\\infty $" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125714 :tutorial/content "Until now, we have considered limits at individual points: \n\n$\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} f(x)$ is the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ approaches the point $a.$ \n\nHowever, the idea of a limit extends beyond individual points. For instance, it might also be the case that $f(x)$ approaches some value as $x$ gets bigger and bigger, growing without bound.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x} + 1$ whose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/71/71fdfd68ce32097cac17da935f0422b471b28d804f4108c62783d0ffc1e96f24.png)\n\nOn the right side of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets bigger and bigger, $f(x)$ approaches the horizontal asymptote $y=1.$ In other words, as $x$ increases to infinity ($\\infty$), the value of $f(x)$ approaches a limit of $1.$ We can write this symbolically as\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\infty } f(x) = 1.\n$$\n\nLikewise, on the left side of the graph, as $x$ decreases to negative infinity ($-\\infty$), the value of $f(x)$ approaches the same horizontal asymptote $y=1.$ Consequently, we also have that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -\\infty } f(x) = 1.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124686 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125504 :step/math-academy-id 655037 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125315 :step/math-academy-id 655038 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125317 :step/math-academy-id 655042 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125505 :step/math-academy-id 655043 :db/ensure [:step/validate]}] "assertions" [[17592186124271 :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find the limit $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).$ \n\n![](images/49/4982eb6e9020e5f6235049fd36a030a69d7b74a0e3ea60323ac8887f4c9bca79.png)"] [17592186124271 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that as $x$ increases to $\\infty,$ the graph of the function moves closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=4.$\n\nTherefore, as $x$ approaches $\\infty,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches $4,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty } f(x) = 4.\n$$"] [17592186124193 :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find  $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} f(x).$ \n\n![](images/c7/c7ee917a93ed51c189979516b9ab0d3232c183921ee277f522f617518ce03fdf.png)"] [17592186124193 :question/worked-solution "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty$, the graph of the function moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=2.$ \n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ approaches the value $2$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -\\infty } f(x) = 2.\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty,$ the graph of the function grows without bound to $\\infty$ as well. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\infty } f(x) = \\infty.\n$$"] ["question-2986a890-b241-5a06-ba6c-578dfc9139a6" :question/id #uuid "2986a890-b241-5a06-ba6c-578dfc9139a6"] ["question-2986a890-b241-5a06-ba6c-578dfc9139a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-337600"] ["question-2986a890-b241-5a06-ba6c-578dfc9139a6" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b35bd1000242bcbc00fcd5e1cd04d8f3b13e563f2df72234b438827949ad4ef5.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Therefore\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}."] ["question-2986a890-b241-5a06-ba6c-578dfc9139a6" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that as $x$ decreases to $-\\infty ,$ the graph of the function moves closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ the function $f(x)$ approaches $2,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=2.\n$$"] ["question-2986a890-b241-5a06-ba6c-578dfc9139a6" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-bebf9261-924a-5e1a-88b8-24fd5ab23e17" :answer-field/id #uuid "bebf9261-924a-5e1a-88b8-24fd5ab23e17"] ["answer-1104f803-e925-52a4-a645-afee67e