e of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty $, the graph of the function grows without bound to $\\infty .$\n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ approaches $\\infty $ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty ,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty ,$\nthe graph of the function moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-2.\n$$"] ["question-be5e5f56-4a60-5c32-8894-f56ae09b72b4" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-ea243ce2-45fd-5cec-817c-777d580842aa" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea243ce2-45fd-5cec-817c-777d580842aa"] ["answer-432e6bf4-e493-5434-ae9a-e0016232e17c" :answer/id #uuid "432e6bf4-e493-5434-ae9a-e0016232e17c"] ["answer-432e6bf4-e493-5434-ae9a-e0016232e17c" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-432e6bf4-e493-5434-ae9a-e0016232e17c" :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-2$ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=-\\infty $"] ["answer-31b42c29-5b27-5782-9407-a827fe74eada" :answer/id #uuid "31b42c29-5b27-5782-9407-a827fe74eada"] ["answer-31b42c29-5b27-5782-9407-a827fe74eada" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-31b42c29-5b27-5782-9407-a827fe74eada" :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-2$ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=2$"] ["answer-4deb45f9-98ed-520e-8fe2-1fe96ff3f473" :answer/id #uuid "4deb45f9-98ed-520e-8fe2-1fe96ff3f473"] ["answer-4deb45f9-98ed-520e-8fe2-1fe96ff3f473" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-4deb45f9-98ed-520e-8fe2-1fe96ff3f473" :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-2$ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty $"] ["answer-cd2fc4cf-c91f-5e2b-ba44-7663cb5c839f" :answer/id #uuid "cd2fc4cf-c91f-5e2b-ba44-7663cb5c839f"] ["answer-cd2fc4cf-c91f-5e2b-ba44-7663cb5c839f" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-cd2fc4cf-c91f-5e2b-ba44-7663cb5c839f" :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=0$ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=\\infty $"] ["answer-dae4ec75-3a91-5668-b380-0a4af9b966a7" :answer/id #uuid "dae4ec75-3a91-5668-b380-0a4af9b966a7"] ["answer-dae4ec75-3a91-5668-b380-0a4af9b966a7" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-dae4ec75-3a91-5668-b380-0a4af9b966a7" :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=-\\infty $"] ["answer-field-ea243ce2-45fd-5cec-817c-777d580842aa" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-ea243ce2-45fd-5cec-817c-777d580842aa" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-ea243ce2-45fd-5cec-817c-777d580842aa" :answer-field/correct "answer-4deb45f9-98ed-520e-8fe2-1fe96ff3f473"] ["answer-field-ea243ce2-45fd-5cec-817c-777d580842aa" :answer-field/choices "answer-432e6bf4-e493-5434-ae9a-e0016232e17c"] ["answer-field-ea243ce2-45fd-5cec-817c-777d580842aa" :answer-field/choices "answer-31b42c29-5b27-5782-9407-a827fe74eada"] ["answer-field-ea243ce2-45fd-5cec-817c-777d580842aa" :answer-field/choices "answer-4deb45f9-98ed-520e-8fe2-1fe96ff3f473"] ["answer-field-ea243ce2-45fd-5cec-817c-777d580842aa" :answer-field/choices "answer-cd2fc4cf-c91f-5e2b-ba44-7663cb5c839f"] ["answer-field-ea243ce2-45fd-5cec-817c-777d580842aa" :answer-field/choices "answer-dae4ec75-3a91-5668-b380-0a4af9b966a7"] ["question-be5e5f56-4a60-5c32-8894-f56ae09b72b4" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-ea243ce2-45fd-5cec-817c-777d580842aa"] [17592186124579 :knowledge-point/questions "question-be5e5f56-4a60-5c32-8894-f56ae09b72b4"] [17592186125714 :tutorial/content "Until now, we have considered limits at individual points: \n\n$\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} f(x)$ is the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ approaches the point $a.$ \n\nHowever, the idea of a limit extends beyond individual points. For instance, it might also be the case that $f(x)$ approaches some value as $x$ gets bigger and bigger, growing without bound.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x} + 1$ whose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/71/71fdfd68ce32097cac17da935f0422b471b28d804f4108c62783d0ffc1e96f24.png)\n\nOn the right side of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets bigger and bigger, $f(x)$ approaches the horizontal asymptote $y=1.$ In other words, as $x$ increases to infinity ($\\infty$), the value of $f(x)$ approaches a limit of $1.$ We can write this symbolically as\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\infty } f(x) = 1.\n$$\n\nLikewise, on the left side of the graph, as $x$ decreases to negative infinity ($-\\infty$), the value of $f(x)$ approaches the same horizontal asymptote $y=1.$ Consequently, we also have that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -\\infty } f(x) = 1.\n$$"] [17592186125504 :step/math-academy-id 655037] [17592186125315 :step/math-academy-id 655038] [17592186125317 :step/math-academy-id 655042] [17592186125505 :step/math-academy-id 655043] [17592186124686 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0]] "retractions" [[17592186124271 :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x)$. Find the limit $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)$.\n\n![](images/49/4982eb6e9020e5f6235049fd36a030a69d7b74a0e3ea60323ac8887f4c9bca79.png)"] [17592186124271 :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that as $x$ increases to $\\infty$, the graph of the function moves closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=4$.\n\nTherefore, as $x$ approaches $\\infty$, the function $f(x)$ approaches $4$, and we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty} f(x) = 4\n$$"] [17592186124193 :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x)$. Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} f(x)$.\n\n![](images/c7/c7ee917a93ed51c189979516b9ab0d3232c183921ee277f522f617518ce03fdf.png)"] [17592186124193 :question/worked-solution "On the left side of the graph, we see that as the values of $x$ decrease and approach $-\\infty$, the graph of the function moves closer and closer to the horizontal asymptote $y=2$.\n\nConsequently, $f(x)$ approaches the value $2$ as $x$ approaches $-\\infty$, and we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -\\infty} f(x) = 2\n$$\n\nOn the right side of the graph, as the values of $x$ increase and approach $\\infty$, the graph of the function grows without bound to $\\infty$ as well. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\infty} f(x) = \\infty\n$$"] [17592186125714 :tutorial/content "Until now, we have considered limits at individual points:\n\n> $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to a} f(x)$ is the value that $f(x)$ approaches as $x$ approaches the point $a$.\n\nHowever, the idea of a limit extends beyond individual points. For instance, it might also be the case that $f(x)$ approaches some value as $x$ gets bigger and bigger, growing without bound.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x} + 1\n$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/71/71fdfd68ce32097cac17da935f0422b471b28d804f4108c62783d0ffc1e96f24.png)\n\nOn the right side of the graph, we see that as $x$ gets bigger and bigger, $f(x)$ approaches the horizontal asymptote $y=1$. In other words, as $x$ increases to infinity ($\\infty$), the value of $f(x)$ approaches a limit of $1$. We can write this symbolically as\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\infty} f(x) = 1\n$$\n\nLikewise, on the left side of the graph, as $x$ decreases to negative infinity ($-\\infty$), the value of $f(x)$ approaches the same horizontal asymptote $y=1$. Consequently, we also have that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to -\\infty} f(x) = 1\n$$"] [17592186125504 :step/math-academy-id 285] [17592186125315 :step/math-academy-id 11991] [17592186125317 :step/math-academy-id 12028] [17592186125505 :step/math-academy-id 286]]}]